      "message": "Hi Sergiu,\n\nThanks for putting together the PARDISO integration and running the comprehensive BAL benchmark suite. The performance results look very promising.\n\nBecause this is a large change that touches the public API, ordering pipeline, preprocessor, and linear solvers, I\u0027d like to do this review in stages: first aligning on the high-level architecture, public API, and CL structure before diving into implementation details.\n\nBelow is a summary of the architectural changes I\u0027d like to see and the reasoning behind them.\n\n\n1. Unstack from CL 28560 (Covariance) and Split into 3 Smaller CLs\n\nRight now, CL 28140 (PARDISO) is stacked on top of CL 28560 (MKL Sparse QR Covariance), which introduces the CMake MKL support, MKL_SPARSE enum, and several mkl_* helper files.\n\nSince MKL\u0027s Sparse QR is not rank-revealing and does not expose the triangular factor R, let\u0027s set the covariance CL aside for now and make PARDISO standalone on top of master. Also, please remove docs/mkl-pardiso-report.md from the git tree (putting the benchmark results in the CL description or a GitHub issue/PR comment is great, but we shouldn\u0027t check benchmark scripts and reports into docs/). If we do decide to go down this route, we should consider doing this during the public API integration time.\n\nTo make the code easier to review, please break this work into 3 focused CLs:\n\n- CL 1: Add LinearSolverOrderingType::AUTO (preparatory CL)\n  Introduce AUTO as the default Solver::Options::linear_solver_ordering_type and resolve it per backend in the preprocessor (details in Section 3 below).\n\n- CL 2: Core MKL Backend (internal/ceres/mkl_sparse.h \u0026 .cc) + SparseCholesky Integration + Unit Tests\n  CMakeLists.txt, cmake/config.h.in, and CeresLapackInt in internal/ceres/lapack.h.\n  internal/ceres/mkl_sparse.h and internal/ceres/mkl_sparse.cc implementing PARDISO and MklSparseCholesky.\n  Wiring MKL_SPARSE into SparseCholesky::Create and sparse_cholesky_test.cc / mkl_sparse_test.cc.\n\n- CL 3: Public API, Preprocessor/Ordering Integration, CLI Flags, Docs \u0026 System Tests\n  include/ceres/types.h, include/ceres/solver.h, solver.cc, trust_region_preprocessor.cc, reorder_program.cc.\n  examples/bundle_adjuster.cc, docs/source/, and high-level tests (linear_solver_test.cc, solver_test.cc, system_test.cc, reorder_program_test.cc, visibility_based_preconditioner_test.cc).\n\n\n2. Consolidate MKL into a Single Backend File Pair (internal/ceres/mkl_sparse.h \u0026 .cc)\n\nFor all other sparse linear algebra backends in Ceres, we isolate the backend wrapper inside a single header/source pair:\n- internal/ceres/suitesparse.h / .cc\n- internal/ceres/accelerate_sparse.h / .cc\n- internal/ceres/eigensparse.h / .cc\n- internal/ceres/cuda_sparse_cholesky.h / .cc\n\nAcross CL 28560 and CL 28140, the MKL code is currently spread across 8+ file pairs (mkl_utils, mkl_diagnostics, mkl_sparse_matrix, mkl_pardiso, mkl_pardiso_diagnostics, mkl_ordering, mkl_normal_matrix, lapack_int.h). We should follow the same pattern as the other backends and keep everything in internal/ceres/mkl_sparse.h and internal/ceres/mkl_sparse.cc (and move CeresLapackInt into internal/ceres/lapack.h).\n\nKey Simplification: Drop mkl_spblas.h (sparse_matrix_t) Entirely\n\nNotice that PARDISO (pardisoinit, pardiso, pardiso_64) takes raw CSR arrays and does not use MKL\u0027s Inspector-Executor Sparse BLAS (mkl_spblas.h / sparse_matrix_t) at all. Currently, mkl_sparse_matrix.h/.cc, mkl_normal_matrix.h/.cc, mkl_ordering.h/.cc, and mkl_diagnostics.h/.cc only exist in CL 28140 for two things:\n(a) Multiplying dummy double matrices with mkl_sparse_syrk, mkl_sparse_sp2m, and mkl_sparse_d_add to find the block sparsity graphs of J\u0027J and the Schur complement S in reorder_program.cc.\n(b) Computing A\u0027A via ComputeAtAUsingMkl in DynamicSparseNormalCholeskySolver.\n\nNeither is needed:\n- In reorder_program.cc: Ceres already has CreateBlockJacobian (Eigen::SparseMatrix\u003cint\u003e from Eigen/SparseCore, which is unconditionally available in Ceres even when CERES_USE_EIGEN_SPARSE is off) and already computes the integer block graphs block_hessian \u003d block_jacobian.transpose() * block_jacobian and block_schur_complement \u003d F.transpose() * F - F.transpose() * E * E.transpose() * F in 5 lines of code (see Section 4 below).\n- In DynamicSparseNormalCholeskySolver: MKL_SPARSE can simply use the existing Eigen SparseMatrix A\u0027A path that EIGEN_SPARSE, ACCELERATE_SPARSE, and CUDA_SPARSE already use.\n\nDropping mkl_spblas.h eliminates mkl_sparse_matrix, mkl_normal_matrix, mkl_ordering, and mkl_diagnostics completely. Also, please replace the 475-line mkl_pardiso_diagnostics.h/.cc with a small (~30-line) error-code-to-string helper inside mkl_sparse.cc (similar to accelerate_sparse.cc and cuda_sparse_cholesky.cc).\n\n\n3. Public API \u0026 Ordering Design (include/ceres/solver.h \u0026 include/ceres/types.h)\n\nMost Ceres users are not experts in sparse direct solver internals or fill-reducing orderings; they just want great solution quality and performance out of the box. With that in mind, we should make four adjustments to the public API changes:\n\nA. Remove Solver::Options::use_two_level_factorization\nWe should not expose PARDISO-specific iparm tuning parameters (iparm[23]) in Solver::Options (or LinearSolver::Options / Preconditioner::Options). It leaks backend internals into the public API and requires 65+ lines of tricky validation rules interacting with solver types, dynamic sparsity, and ordering types. Please remove use_two_level_factorization and stick with PARDISO\u0027s standard factorization (iparm[23] \u003d 0). If we think this is the right thing to do, then we should just make it the default thing that we do rather than exposing it unnecessarily.\n\nB. Introduce LinearSolverOrderingType::AUTO as the Default Ordering Type\nIn your documentation changes, you noted an important tension:\n- Ceres currently defaults Solver::Options::linear_solver_ordering_type to AMD.\n- While AMD is the best default for SUITE_SPARSE, EIGEN_SPARSE, and ACCELERATE_SPARSE, PARDISO\u0027s own native default in pardisoinit for SPD matrices is Nested Dissection (NESDIS), and when num_threads \u003e 1 PARDISO uses Parallel Nested Dissection (iparm[1] \u003d 3). PARDISO\u0027s minimum degree (iparm[1] \u003d 0) is sequential and can suffer from both slow symbolic analysis and severe fill-in on large problems (such as overflowing 32-bit factor nonzeros on final-13682).\n\nRather than defaulting to AMD and warning users in the documentation to manually switch linear_solver_ordering_type to NESDIS when using MKL_SPARSE, let\u0027s add AUTO to LinearSolverOrderingType in include/ceres/types.h and make it the default in Solver::Options:\n\n  enum LinearSolverOrderingType {\n    // Automatically choose the fill-reducing ordering algorithm based on\n    // the sparse linear algebra library.\n    AUTO,\n    // Approximate Minimum Degree.\n    AMD,\n    // Nested Dissection.\n    NESDIS\n  };\n\nIn the preprocessor (SetupLinearSolver), when linear_solver_ordering_type \u003d\u003d AUTO, Ceres can resolve it based on sparse_linear_algebra_library_type:\n- SUITE_SPARSE, EIGEN_SPARSE, ACCELERATE_SPARSE -\u003e AMD\n- MKL_SPARSE -\u003e NESDIS\n- CUDA_SPARSE -\u003e AMD (or NESDIS, since cuDSS uses nested dissection internally)\n\nThis gives users the best out-of-the-box ordering for whichever backend they select without needing to understand AMD vs. NESDIS. (In Solver::Summary, we can report the resolved ordering type just like we do for linear_solver_type_given / linear_solver_type_used.)\n\nC. Remove StablySortOrderingByGroup and Keep Existing linear_solver_ordering Semantics\nCurrently in include/ceres/solver.h, Ceres has two rules for user-supplied ParameterBlockOrdering groups:\n1. SUITE_SPARSE + AMD: Uses CAMD to respect all elimination groups.\n2. All other backends (EIGEN_SPARSE, ACCELERATE_SPARSE, CUDA_SPARSE, and SUITE_SPARSE + NESDIS):\n   - For SPARSE_NORMAL_CHOLESKY and CGNR (SUBSET): linear_solver_ordering is ignored and an unconstrained AMD or NESDIS ordering is used.\n   - For Schur-type solvers (SPARSE_SCHUR, etc.): only the lowest group is used to define the E blocks for the Schur complement, and unconstrained AMD or NESDIS orders the Schur complement (F blocks).\n\nD. Keep SUITE_SPARSE as the Primary Default Backend [This I am not entirely sure about]\n\nIn Solver::Options::sparse_linear_algebra_library_type, please keep SUITE_SPARSE ahead of MKL_SPARSE:\n  SUITE_SPARSE \u003e MKL_SPARSE \u003e ACCELERATE_SPARSE \u003e EIGEN_SPARSE \u003e NO_SPARSE\nBecause WITH_MKL is ON by default in CMakeLists.txt, putting MKL_SPARSE first silently changes the default sparse solver for anyone building Ceres on a system where MKL is installed (which is very commonly installed just to provide BLAS/LAPACK for SuiteSparse).\n\nOn the other hand, if we go with good performance out of the book, it maybe better to use it if it\u0027s available if we are confident it always does better than SuiteSparse. We should discuss this some more.\n\n\n[The following bit of feedback from gemini I did not grok, so lets start with something conservative first and then see if this is needed]\n\nCL 28140 introduces a third behavior for MKL_SPARSE (StablySortOrderingByGroup), which computes an unconstrained PARDISO ordering (AMD or NESDIS) and then post-sorts the permutation by user group IDs.\n\n\nWhy we shouldn\u0027t do this: Post-sorting an unconstrained AMD or Nested Dissection permutation by group ID invalidates the fill-reducing property of the ordering. Unconstrained AMD/ND places high-degree separator nodes at the end under the assumption that their neighbors are eliminated first; forcibly moving a node to the front because it belongs to group 0, without constrained degree updates during elimination like CAMD, can cause catastrophic fill-in.\n\nPlease remove StablySortOrderingByGroup and have MKL_SPARSE follow the exact same rule as EIGEN_SPARSE and ACCELERATE_SPARSE.\n\n\n4. Unifying Block Ordering in reorder_program.cc [This should perhaps be its own change]\n\nIn ReorderSchurComplementColumnsUsingMkl, your observation that sorting the E blocks (points) by the earliest F block (camera) they share a residual with significantly speeds up SchurEliminator::Eliminate on large problems is spot on: SUITE_SPARSE + AMD got that grouping as a side effect of running CAMD on the full Jacobian, whereas ReorderSchurComplementColumnsUsingEigen left the E blocks in their original order.\n\nNotice that in reorder_program.cc, both SPARSE_NORMAL_CHOLESKY and SPARSE_SCHUR block ordering can be unified cleanly across backends without any MKL-specific graph construction:\n\n- For SPARSE_NORMAL_CHOLESKY (ReorderProgramForSparseCholesky):\n  OrderingForSparseNormalCholeskyUsingEigenSparse already builds the symmetric block Hessian graph using Eigen::SparseMatrix\u003cint\u003e:\n    const SparseMatrix block_jacobian \u003d\n        CreateBlockJacobian(tsm_block_jacobian_transpose);\n    const SparseMatrix block_hessian \u003d\n        block_jacobian.transpose() * block_jacobian;\n  MKL_SPARSE can use the exact same block_hessian sparsity pattern and pass its CSR structure (outerIndexPtr(), innerIndexPtr()) to MklSparse::Ordering(...).\n\n- For SPARSE_SCHUR (ReorderProgramForSchurTypeLinearSolver):\n  When not using SUITE_SPARSE + AMD (CAMD), ReorderSchurComplementColumnsUsingEigen already builds the block Schur complement pattern using Eigen::SparseMatrix\u003cint\u003e:\n    const SparseMatrix block_jacobian \u003d\n        CreateBlockJacobian(*tsm_block_jacobian_transpose);\n    const SparseMatrix E \u003d\n        block_jacobian.block(0, 0, num_rows, size_of_first_elimination_group);\n    const SparseMatrix F \u003d\n        block_jacobian.block(0,\n                             size_of_first_elimination_group,\n                             num_rows,\n                             num_cols - size_of_first_elimination_group);\n    const SparseMatrix block_schur_complement \u003d\n        F.transpose() * F - F.transpose() * E * E.transpose() * F;\n  Instead of having separate ReorderSchurComplementColumnsUsingEigen and ReorderSchurComplementColumnsUsingMkl functions, we can have a single function that forms block_schur_complement, computes the F-block permutation using the active backend (EIGEN_SPARSE, MKL_SPARSE, or even SUITE_SPARSE with NESDIS), and then applies both the F-block ordering and your E-block locality ordering for all of them.\n\n\n5. Feature Parity: Supporting use_mixed_precision_solves\n\nAll four of our existing sparse Cholesky backends (SuiteSparse, EigenSparse, AccelerateSparse, CudaSparseCholesky) support single-precision factorization for Solver::Options::use_mixed_precision_solves.\n\nSince PARDISO natively supports single-precision factorization via iparm[27] \u003d 1 (with float values and float RHS/solution arrays), templating MklSparseCholesky\u003cScalar\u003e on double and float (just like AppleAccelerateCholesky\u003cScalar\u003e in accelerate_sparse.h and CudaSparseCholesky\u003cScalar\u003e in cuda_sparse_cholesky.h) should be very straightforward. Doing so will make MKL_SPARSE completely uniform with the other backends and eliminate the special-case error checks and test exclusions in solver.cc, sparse_cholesky.cc, and system_test.cc.\n\nSameer",
